gl: give framebuffers a stencil-only texture, without a depth buffer

Each monitor has one stencil texture, attached to its blur framebuffer and
to its work buffers, which blur's discard pass (ignore_alpha) draws through.
It was made GL_DEPTH24_STENCIL8, but nothing reads or writes depth: depth
testing is turned off as the texture is attached. GLES 3.2 has stencil-only
textures (GL_STENCIL_INDEX8), so on a 3.2 context the texture is that,
attached at GL_STENCIL_ATTACHMENT; the GLES 3.0 fallback keeps
GL_DEPTH24_STENCIL8, which 3.0 needs for a stencil texture.

Mesa's iris keeps depth and stencil apart, so the depth part alone was
48 MiB a monitor at 3840x2400; the 10 MiB stencil stays.

In a nested Hyprland at 3840x2400 at rest (Intel, iris), the render node's
DRM fdinfo said 375.1 to 375.3 MiB before and 321.0 to 321.2 after, in runs
that alternated between the two builds (two of each four also held a 40 MiB
buffer of Mesa's, 415 before and 361 after). With a layer rule of blur and
ignore_alpha on every layer, so that the stencil is drawn through, pictures
of the desktop, the bar's panels, menus and overlays are the same before
and after but for the clock's minute.

--- a/src/render/gl/GLFramebuffer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/gl/GLFramebuffer.cpp
@@ -55,8 +55,16 @@
 
     if (m_stencilTex && m_stencilTex->ok()) {
         m_stencilTex->bind();
-        glTexImage2D(GL_TEXTURE_2D, 0, GL_DEPTH24_STENCIL8, w, h, 0, GL_DEPTH_STENCIL, GL_UNSIGNED_INT_24_8, nullptr);
-        glFramebufferTexture2D(GL_FRAMEBUFFER, GL_DEPTH_STENCIL_ATTACHMENT, GL_TEXTURE_2D, m_stencilTex->m_texID, 0);
+        // Only the stencil is ever used (blur's discard pass), with depth testing
+        // off. GLES 3.2 has stencil-only textures; a depth buffer beside the
+        // stencil took 48 MiB a 3840x2400 monitor for nothing.
+        if (g_pHyprOpenGL->m_eglContextVersion == CHyprOpenGLImpl::EGL_CONTEXT_GLES_3_2) {
+            glTexImage2D(GL_TEXTURE_2D, 0, GL_STENCIL_INDEX8, w, h, 0, GL_STENCIL_INDEX, GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE, nullptr);
+            glFramebufferTexture2D(GL_FRAMEBUFFER, GL_STENCIL_ATTACHMENT, GL_TEXTURE_2D, m_stencilTex->m_texID, 0);
+        } else {
+            glTexImage2D(GL_TEXTURE_2D, 0, GL_DEPTH24_STENCIL8, w, h, 0, GL_DEPTH_STENCIL, GL_UNSIGNED_INT_24_8, nullptr);
+            glFramebufferTexture2D(GL_FRAMEBUFFER, GL_DEPTH_STENCIL_ATTACHMENT, GL_TEXTURE_2D, m_stencilTex->m_texID, 0);
+        }
 
         glDisable(GL_DEPTH_TEST);
         glDepthMask(GL_FALSE);
